    },
    "description": "The Warehouse Scheduler publishes an InventorySnapshot every hour between 02:00 and 04:00, and the Reporting Import consumer writes those snapshots into the merchandising database. By 04:15 the nightly merchandise report must contain current stock levels, so a missing stream makes the stale data business-visible even though every host and broker remains healthy. Individual snapshot failures already create failed-message records, but the current alert set only reacts to explicit errors. The operations team needs to be notified when expected arrivals stop, while a scheduled warehouse holiday and the normal gap after 04:00 must not generate pages. Multiple scheduler instances may publish to the same logical flow, so the check must reflect the flow's aggregate arrival cadence rather than one host's status. The design must distinguish an absent flow from a flow that is still arriving but contains a problematic payload, and it must identify the Reporting Import owner when it raises the alert.",
    "decision_statement": "Whether the inventory snapshot flow should have a deadline-based absence check that alerts on silence even though all processes remain healthy.",
    "notes": "Primary watchman because the decision is bounded detection of a silent flow against its business deadline; message-store stays as the near miss since querying durable copies is the plausible alternative gap-detection design."
